@meta
use_case: Supplier credit limit increase requests raised by sales, about sixty a month, each asking to raise one customer's credit limit
author: thechrisoneil
date: 2026-09-22
approver: Credit Manager
exit_criterion: Shut down if approved increases that go to write-off exceeds 3 in any rolling six months

@assumptions
- The ERP exposes each request with the customer account number, the current limit, the requested limit and the sales justification text by query
- Payment history is available as structured aged-debt and days-beyond-terms figures per customer, not as a narrative
- Customer size is available as trailing twelve month billed revenue on the customer account; this is the denominator used to judge whether an increase is large relative to the customer
- Assumed a routine band of a requested increase at or below 25 percent of the current limit and at or below 10 percent of trailing twelve month revenue; confirm with the Credit Manager
- Assumed a single-approver cap of 250000 USD on the resulting limit, above which the Finance Director countersigns; confirm with finance
- Assumed the sector knowledge the hard cases need is held by the Credit Manager and a sector risk note, and that the loop proposes rather than decides
- Assumed no external credit bureau feed is available in the first version, so the loop reasons only on internal payment history
- The sales justification is externally written text and is screened before it reaches any model
- Assumed the five day figure is calendar days from request submission to analyst decision, measured in the ERP timestamps
- Assumed requests inside the routine band are decided by the existing ERP limit rule outside this loop and never enter it, leaving roughly twenty of the sixty a month as the judgment population; confirm the split with the Credit Manager
- Assumed the routine band thresholds are reviewed monthly, because a band that drifts silently moves rule-shaped work onto a model and moves judgment work onto a rule

@unit
definition: One credit limit increase request for one customer account
kind: rule
two_people_test: Two analysts given the same ERP export produce the same list of requests, because the ERP issues one request number per submission against one account
fanout: 8
fanout_reason: Bounded by what one credit analyst reviews and signs in a day, not by machine capacity

@steps
1 | pull_requests | mechanical | code | Retrieve open increase requests with account, current limit, requested limit and justification | batch
2 | build_profile | mechanical | code | Join aged debt, days beyond terms and trailing twelve month revenue, and screen the justification text | batch
3 | rank_and_cap | coordination | code | Admit only requests outside the routine band, order by requested increase and age, and cap the day | batch
4 | intake_gate | gate | code | Refuse a request lacking payment history or an account on credit hold | unit
5 | assess_sector_fit | thinking | model | Decide whether the sector and trading pattern justify an increase this large for a customer this size | unit
6 | recompute_exposure | test | code | Recompute increase ratio, exposure against revenue and worst days beyond terms from source data | unit
7 | result_gate | gate | code | Refuse when the recomputation disagrees or the assessment cites no payment figure | unit
8 | write_record | mechanical | code | Write the request, the profile, the recomputation and the assessment, then checksum it | unit
9 | route_request | coordination | code | Write a proposal assigned to the named approver | unit

@gates
3 | more than 60 percent of a calendar month's requests fall outside the routine band, or the admitted count exceeds the daily cap of 8 | The Credit Manager reviews the routine band thresholds against that month's requests and reissues the band within 2 working days before the queue is worked
4 | fewer than 6 months of payment history on the account, or the account carries an open credit hold flag, or unpaid invoices over 60 days past due exceed 0 | The Credit Analyst returns the request to the originating salesperson naming the missing history or the hold, with a 3 working day resubmission date
7 | the recomputed increase ratio differs from the assessed ratio by more than 0.01, or the assessment quotes no days-beyond-terms figure, or the resulting limit exceeds 250000 USD without a Finance Director countersignature | The Credit Manager opens the request and decides on the payment history directly
9 | record checksum differs from the checksum written at step 8, or the approver field is never set to a machine account | Hold the request and have the ERP systems owner establish who edited the record and when

@evidence
writer_step: 8
artifact: The request as submitted, the routine band test that admitted it, the payment history profile, the recomputed exposure figures, and the sector assessment with the figures it cites
integrity: SHA-256 written with the record and re-checked before the request is routed to the approver

@kpis
cost per completed decision | cost | unmeasured
share of requests decided by the routine band rule outside this loop | cost | unmeasured
approved increases that later go to write-off | quality | unmeasured
days from request submission to decision | throughput | 5 days
refusal rate at intake | control | not applicable before this loop
requests admitted outside the routine band per month | control | unmeasured
